{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And you shall count its foreskin regarding its fruit</b> — and you shall stop up its stopping, it shall be stopped up and hidden from being enjoyed. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:23), and he explained well. If so, then 'uncircumcised of heart' (Ezekiel 44:9) means a closed heart, as it says, 'and I will tear open the closure of their heart' (Hosea 13:8). Similarly, 'their ear is uncircumcised' (Jeremiah 6:10) — it is closed and stopped up, sound does not enter it. And 'uncircumcised lips' (Exodus 6:12) — closed lips, for the speech impediment is a stoppage and closure in the tendons of the tongue, and sometimes in the tendons of the lips so that they do not open properly. And speech is called an opening: 'Open your mouth for the mute' (Proverbs 31:8), 'Job opened his mouth' (Job 3:1), 'from the opening of my lips uprightness' (Proverbs 8:6), 'from the embrace of your bosom, guard the openings of your mouth' (Micah 7:5). Scripture mentioned a stoppage regarding the fruit that comes within three years, to prohibit it in benefit. And it does not speak thus regarding other prohibitions of benefit, because the emergence of the fruit at its beginning is called an opening, as it says, 'the blossom has opened' (Song of Songs 7:13). Therefore it says, 'three years it shall be to you forbidden,' as if the fruits are closed up in the trees — they did not put forth a flower nor open a blossom. And the reason for this commandment is to honor HaShem from the first of all our produce, from the fruit of the tree and the produce of the vineyard, and not to eat of them until we bring every fruit of one year as a celebration for HaShem. Now, the fruit within three years is not fit to offer before the honored HaShem, because it is meager, and the tree does not give in its fruit good taste or fragrance within three years, and most of them do not produce fruit at all until the fourth year. Therefore we wait for them all and do not taste of them until we bring from the tree we planted all its first good fruit as holy before HaShem, and there it shall be eaten, and they shall praise the name of HaShem. This commandment resembles the commandment of first fruits. And the truth of the matter is further that the fruit at the beginning of the tree's planting is very moist, exceedingly sticky, harmful to the body and not good to eat — like a fish that has no scales (Leviticus 11:9) — and the foods forbidden in the Torah are also bad for the body. And the Rav gave a reason for this as well in the Guide for the Perplexed (3:37), as he did for most of the commandments: the sorcerers and magicians at that time would perform certain acts of sorcery at the time of planting the trees, and they believed that through them the tree would quickly produce its fruit before its known time according to the way of the world, and when it came they would bring the fruit before the idolatry they practiced in the name of that sorcery. Therefore the Torah commanded the prohibition of the fruit that comes before three years, so that they would not come to perform those evil deeds, for most trees produce fruit in the fourth year; and that we eat it before HaShem — the opposite of their eating it before idolatry."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ושלא נטעום' appears to be a scribal contraction of 'ושלא נטעם מהם'; translated hyper-literally as 'and we do not taste of them.' The citation 'ואקרע סגור לבם' (Hosea 13:8) follows Ramban's Hebrew text, which differs slightly from the Masoretic Text; translated exactly as he quotes it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:38.284793941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}